Install and enable this profile
===============================

1. Install prerequisites

   * authselect
   * pam-u2f  (module and pamu2fcfg utility)
   * sssd     (if you use remote identity/auth providers)

   On Fedora/RHEL:
     sudo dnf install authselect pam-u2f pamu2fcfg sssd

2. Copy profile files to authselect custom profiles directory

     sudo mkdir -p /etc/authselect/custom/sssd-u2f
     sudo cp -a . /etc/authselect/custom/sssd-u2f/

3. Generate FIDO2 SSH key files

   This profile uses sshformat, so keys are generated with ssh-keygen and
   stored in ~/.ssh/. The same files work for both system auth and SSH.

   Standard keys (PIN-capable, e.g. YubiKey 5C NFC, 5C Nano):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_first
     # touch key when prompted

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_second
     # touch backup key when prompted

   YubiKey Bio — early editions (fingerprint enforced by firmware, no PIN fallback):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -f ~/.ssh/u2f_bio
     # touch key and provide fingerprint when prompted

   YubiKey Bio — newer editions (supports PIN fallback):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_bio
     # Also update the bio line in system-auth to:
     #   pam_u2f.so cue sshformat userverification=1 pinverification=0 authfile=.ssh/u2f_bio

   Keep file permissions restrictive:

     chmod 600 ~/.ssh/u2f_*

4. Select the profile

   Basic (U2F only):
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f with-pam-u2f

   With optional features (adjust to your system):
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f with-pam-u2f with-sudo with-mdns4 with-mdns6

   Capture your existing profile options to preserve them:
     mapfile -t OPTS < <(authselect current | awk '/^- with-/ {gsub("^- ",""); print}')
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f "${OPTS[@]}" with-pam-u2f

5. Apply and verify

     sudo authselect apply-changes
     authselect current

6. Test before logging out

   Always test sudo and a new terminal login before closing your current
   session. If something goes wrong, revert with:

     sudo authselect select sssd --force

Notes
-----

* The profile does not build pam_u2f from source. Install via your
  package manager.

* The routing logic in system-auth uses paired guard+key blocks (each guard
  jumps exactly success=1 over its partner). Do not insert lines between a
  guard and its pam_u2f.so partner, and do not change the {include if} tags
  on one without changing the other.

* If you add a new key, add a complete guard+key pair — one for the
  touch-only (sudo) path and one for the PIN/bio (GDM) path.

* Review system-auth and password-auth before enabling on any production host.
  In particular, verify the list of login services in the guard lines matches
  the GDM PAM files present on your system:
    ls /etc/pam.d/gdm-*
